WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:05.710
Comedy duo Tim and Eric generally drum up
controversy simply because their comedy is

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:11.869
so bizarre, and it may not always sit well
with a typical stand-up comedy fan.

00:00:11.869 --> 00:00:18.150
But the work of Tim Heidecker and Eric Wareheim
has taken some strange turns, and their various

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:23.610
TV projects have made plenty of people genuinely
irate for many reasons.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:27.160
Here’s the untold truth of Tim &amp; Eric.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.210
Think Of The Children

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:34.840
The fifth season of Tim and Eric Awesome Show,
Great Job! on Adult Swim explored some pretty

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.950
dark comedic territory.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.480
On at least one occasion, Tim and Eric got
into some trouble for it.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:48.480
A sketch called "Dream Cream" cast a young
boy to attend a dream-sequence barbecue with

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:56.120
Pierre, a recurring character who's really
into barbecued meat and meeting your dad.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.920
“Hi beautiful boy.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.450
Welcome to my dream.”

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:06.409
During a questionable scene, the kid eats
hot dogs and turkey legs out of Pierre's hands,

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:10.430
which made the kid's parents very uncomfortable.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:15.090
The episode only aired once before the parents
complained, forcing the show to replace the

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.029
kid with an animated figure instead.

00:01:18.029 --> 00:01:22.609
During their first Reddit AMA, Tim and Eric
said that it was just easier to make the change

00:01:22.609 --> 00:01:30.009
than to hurt anyone.

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:31.979
Taking Advantage

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:37.659
During the run of Awesome Show, the most common
allegation made by viewers and critics was

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:42.799
that the duo took advantage of the mentally
ill or intellectually disabled.

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:49.649
But while much of the show's comedy relies
on the unusual personalities of amateur actors,

00:01:49.649 --> 00:01:55.509
only one of their stable of repeat guests
has ever said anything about not being treated

00:01:55.509 --> 00:01:56.959
professionally.

00:01:56.959 --> 00:02:02.700
Alien-enthusiast and puppeteer David Liebe
Hart has occasionally accused Tim and Eric

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.500
of not paying him enough or not taking him
on their national tours.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.540
And those allegations were dropped pretty
quickly when Hart became a regular on Tim

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.040
and Eric's Check It Out!

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.710
With Dr. Steve Brule.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.750
“I’m afraid of puppets.”

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.350
“Well, you don’t have to be afraid of
them.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.820
My puppets are very well trained.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.820
They won’t scare you.”

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.530
“No way Jose.”

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:31.940
Other strange appearances have included The
Room’s Tommy Wiseau, and Robert Axelrod,

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.310
the voice of Power Rangers’ Lord Zedd.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.160
“Two of this weeks movies to watch.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.830
I saw a dand stranger.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.330
I give it two kisses and three wet mouths.”

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.560
“I am Lord Zedd, emperor of all I see.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.754
You have failed to complete the mission assigned
to you.”

00:02:47.754 --> 00:02:52.370
The unusual guest stars find themselves in
pretty good company, but the duo do admit

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.790
to an unusual casting process.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.500
They told their 2012 audience at the Sundance
Film Festival,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.600
"We dig through to the bottom of the pile
[of headshots] and find them, then give them

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.070
lots of lines."

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:09.310
Not everyone stuck around to ask questions
at Sundance, though...

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.410
Sundance Fury

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:14.410
It's not just parents that have been upset
with Tim and Eric.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:19.910
According to Entertainment Weekly, at least
one-third of the audience left their 2012

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:23.870
Sundance screening of Tim and Eric's Billion
Dollar Movie.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.340
Many of the remaining audience members were
apparently hostile, accusing the filmmakers

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.720
of taking drugs, or genuinely trying to upset
paying audiences.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.780
Tim and Eric fired right back, however…

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.260
“Are you trying out new material for Q &amp; A’s.”

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:48.340
...and the film went on to earn a respectable
38% on Rotten Tomatoes; a solid score for

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.320
a movie that was intentionally unwatchable.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.880
“Shhhhhhrrriiiimmmmmppppp!”

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:55.990
“Shrimp”

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.640
Tiny Hats

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:04.100
Weird as they are, Tim and Eric have garnered
the attention of mainstream comedy shows like

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:10.340
Saturday Night Live, making Will Forte, Will
Ferrell, and Fred Armisen all cast members.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:15.650
Because of Tim and Eric's unofficial associations
with SNL, it's not too surprising that, in

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:21.079
2012, the long-running sketch show was accused
of stealing a popular Awesome Show sketch

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:23.320
concerning tiny hats.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:28.650
While Heidecker himself wasn't upset, he called
the incident "very interesting," and said

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:33.330
that he looked forward to seeing the repercussions
on the internet.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.169
Go With The Flo

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:41.310
Even if you don't recognize Tim and Eric themselves,
they do regularly work with a face you have

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:45.069
to recognize: Flo from Progressive Insurance.

00:04:45.069 --> 00:04:49.819
Before she started grinning maniacally for
Progressive, actress Stephanie Courtney provided

00:04:49.819 --> 00:04:55.770
the monstrous voice of Joy Peters, the oppressive
and cruel wife on their original Adult Swim

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.150
show, Tom Goes to the Mayor.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.820
“Nice work, Idiot.”

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:06.599
She would also appear as a music video dancer
in the same show, a casting director on Awesome

00:05:06.599 --> 00:05:11.550
Show, and even followed the duo when they
made an appearance on The Simpsons, backup

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:16.950
singing about food blogs during Tim and Eric's
rap … which they also wrote.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:27.510
(Tim and Eric Simpson's episode song playing)

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.940
Eric Dated A Much Younger Woman

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:38.830
Fans of 2010-era MTV may remember a two-season,
faux-reality show called My Life as Liz, starring

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:43.919
high schooler Liz Lee as she ventured off
to college from small-town Texas.

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:50.150
Not long after the show was dropped in 2013,
22-year old Lee was spotted just about everywhere

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.610
with 37-year old Eric Wareheim.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:58.419
While the relationship didn't seem to last
long, both MTV-hungry teens and Tim and Eric

00:05:58.419 --> 00:06:05.169
devotees savagely criticized the relationship
for the huge age disparity between the two.

00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:07.330
Tim's Secret Life

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:13.710
It's not that Tim Heidecker is hiding any
deep dark secrets (probably), but he only

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:19.999
recently revealed that he has a wife and child—a
big surprise to his legions of fans.

00:06:19.999 --> 00:06:26.270
While Vice made brief mention of this back
in 2013, the full revelation came in the lyrics

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:32.520
of his most recent album, In Glendale, a serious
soft-rock album about his life in California.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:38.900
Previous Heidecker albums have been exclusively
about bodily excretions, Herman Cain campaign

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:46.289
songs, or parodies of basically everything
else, so In Glendale's honesty came as a shock.

00:06:46.289 --> 00:06:50.699
Many fans still aren't sure about what it
all means, especially since Heidecker usually

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:56.089
maintains an Andy Kaufman-like wall of inauthenticity
about him.

00:06:56.089 --> 00:06:57.969
Tim Was Stabbed

00:06:57.969 --> 00:07:03.360
The only other time that Heidecker has been
truly and transparently honest about his actual

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.639
life was when he was almost killed in 2006.

00:07:07.639 --> 00:07:13.409
In both his now-defunct personal blog, and
a later live storytelling session, Tim mapped

00:07:13.409 --> 00:07:15.310
out the deadly assault.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:21.659
The attack was made by a neighbor's son who
was evidently high on PCP, and included some

00:07:21.659 --> 00:07:26.370
pretty gory photos of where he was knifed
twice in the shoulder.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:31.440
“Umm, I have to this day no memory of the
actual impact of the knife which is kind of

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.400
a fascinating thing to me, that, um, I have
no, you know, the the adrenaline running through

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:43.309
my, through my veins, uh, as I’m being,
as I’m running up fountain I think prevented

00:07:43.309 --> 00:07:45.250
any feeling of pain.”

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:50.169
After being stabbed and making a sprint through
the streets, Heidecker ran into a bar for

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:55.259
assistance, where the assailant was tackled
by a group of patrons.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:01.680
“The real Heidecker can never die because
he is a child.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:07.599
Who is this?

00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:09.139
He is gonna be alive.

00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:11.159
This is just a crank.”

00:08:11.159 --> 00:08:13.149
The Mystery Of Dr. Brule

00:08:13.149 --> 00:08:17.669
“Hey I’m Dr. Steve Brule with some of
the Brules Rules.

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:20.469
Does your milk taste sour when you take a
sip of it?

00:08:20.469 --> 00:08:22.569
Check the expiration date dummy.”

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:30.120
One of the duo's most popular characters is
Dr. Steve Brule, played by John C. Reilly.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:36.210
The good doctor is so popular, even viewers
who despise Tim and Eric can appreciate Brule's

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:38.710
off-kilter exploration of modern life.

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:39.800
“Ham”

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:53.340
“H, the letter of A, bunch of other letters
S S S S P P P P, the end.”

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:58.640
After many appearances on Awesome Show, Dr.
Brule got a spinoff and was invited to do

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:05.000
a ton of alt-comedy interviews. The only problem
is that Reilly outright refuses to answer

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:10.000
any questions about the character, instead
representing himself as an acquaintance of

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.000
Brule, in order to maintain the goofy mystery
about Steve.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:20.000
“John that is clearly you. No, he was wearing
glasses, Conan.”

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:27.000
We'll probably never know the influences that
helped create the character, and Reilly's

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:32.600
refusal to disclose a single idea makes him
that much stranger.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.560
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.540
you just saw. And leave us a comment to tell
us which is your favorite Tim &amp; Eric sketch…

